## Network Contract
{# health ends in `+%}` because trim_blocks would otherwise eat the first of the two
   newlines before the protocol section. #}
{% block contract %}{% block health %}### GET /health
Return 200 **only** when the model is fully loaded and the inference endpoint can
serve a request immediately. Return non-200 (e.g. 503) at any point before that.
**If you load the model in a background thread, your /health must block on load
completion before returning 200.** Returning 200 early (before the model is ready)
violates the substrate's readiness contract: the harness stops polling /health
the moment it sees 200 and immediately sends inference traffic.{% endblock +%}

{{ protocol_section }}{% endblock %}


## Decoding
{% block decoding %}{% if decode.kind == "sample" %}
**Decoding.** Sample at temperature {{ "%g" % decode.temperature }}. At each position, apply
the softmax to the model's logits over the full vocabulary{% if decode.temperature != 1.0 %}
divided by {{ "%g" % decode.temperature }}{% endif %} and draw the next token from that
distribution. No top-p, top-k or min-p truncation; no repetition, frequency or presence
penalty; no logit bias. The chat-templated prompt already carries the model's special tokens,
so do not add another BOS. A reply ends when the model emits one of its end-of-turn tokens
(`finish_reason` `stop`, with that token as the last token id served) or when `max_tokens`
tokens have been served or the context window is full (`finish_reason` `length`).

For every generated token, report its log-probability and the most probable alternatives at
that position with their log-probabilities and token ids, as the Network Contract above
specifies. The reported values must be the distribution you sampled from.

**Verification.** For every served token, the reference model is run on the same prefix and
the KL divergence from your reported distribution to the reference's is averaged over all
graded tokens. The run fails if this mean exceeds {% if verification and verification.kind == "report" %}{{ verification.kl_ceiling_str }} nats per token{% else %}a ceiling calibrated on trusted serving stacks{% endif %}.
Your served tokens must be faithful draws from the distribution you report; this is tested
statistically, and a server that reports one distribution and samples from another fails.
The reported alternatives must be the most probable tokens: the run fails if more than
{% if verification and verification.kind == "report" %}{{ verification.outside_limit_pct_str }} percent{% else %}a fraction calibrated on trusted serving stacks{% endif %} of your served tokens are not among the alternatives you reported at
their position.
{%- elif decode.frequency_penalty %}
**Decoding.** Decode greedily after a frequency penalty of {{ "%g" % decode.frequency_penalty }}:
at each position, subtract {{ "%g" % decode.frequency_penalty }} times the number of times a token id
already appears in the reply you are generating from that token's logit, then take the
argmax. Count only this request's output tokens, never the prompt; apply it in fp32
before the softmax. This is the OpenAI `frequency_penalty`, and vLLM and SGLang
implement it this way when given `frequency_penalty={{ "%g" % decode.frequency_penalty }}`
with `temperature=0`. The correctness gate teacher-forces your tokens through a
reference decoded the same way, so any other decode fails it by construction. The
chat-templated prompt already carries the model's special tokens, so do not add
another BOS; strip special tokens from the output, stopping at the end-of-turn token
and never emitting the raw end token (e.g. `<|im_end|>`) in the response text.
{%- else %}
**Decoding.** Decode greedily (argmax, no sampling). The chat-templated prompt already
carries the model's special tokens, so do not add another BOS; strip special tokens from the output,
stopping at the end-of-turn token and never emitting the raw end token (e.g. `<|im_end|>`) in the
response text.
{%- endif %}{% endblock %}


